### Definition: Reference Type vs Object Type

In Java, every reference variable assignment has two distinct types:
```java
Vehicle v = new SportsCar();
```
* **Compile-Time Reference Type (`Vehicle`)**: The declared type on the left side of the assignment. The compiler inspects this type to decide **which methods and fields can be legally called**.
* **Runtime Object Type (`SportsCar`)**: The concrete class instantiated on the right side using `new`. The JVM inspects this type in heap memory to decide **which overridden method implementation executes**.

**Core Rules Governing References:**
1. **The Compiler Looks at the Reference Type**: You can only call methods that exist in the declared reference class.
2. **The JVM Looks at the Actual Object Type**: When an accessible method is called, the JVM dispatches the subclass version if it was overridden.
3. **Upcasting & Downcasting**: Assigning a child object to a parent reference is **Upcasting** (automatic and safe). Converting a parent reference back to a child type is **downcasting** (requires an explicit cast and can throw `ClassCastException` if the types mismatch).

---

### Demonstrating Reference Boundaries in Code

```java
class Vehicle {
    void start() {
        System.out.println("Vehicle is starting");
    }
}

class SportsCar extends Vehicle {
    @Override
    void start() {
        System.out.println("SportsCar V8 engine roaring to life");
    }

    void activateTurbo() {
        System.out.println("Turbo boost activated!");
    }
}

public class ReferenceTypeDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Parent Reference holding Child Object (Upcasting)
        Vehicle v = new SportsCar();

        // 1. Legal: start() is declared in Vehicle, overridden in SportsCar
        v.start(); // Outputs: SportsCar V8 engine roaring to life

        // 2. Compile error if uncommented:
        // v.activateTurbo(); // Error: Vehicle does not have activateTurbo()

        // 3. Safe Downcasting with type verification
        if (v instanceof SportsCar) {
            SportsCar car = (SportsCar) v;
            car.activateTurbo(); // Now legal!
        }
    }
}
```

**Console Output:**
```text
SportsCar V8 engine roaring to life
Turbo boost activated!
```

---

### Safe Downcasting and Avoiding ClassCastException

Always use `instanceof` before downcasting. If a reference points to a different subclass (e.g. `Truck`), attempting `(SportsCar) v` results in a runtime `ClassCastException`.

activities.codeSnippet:
class Vehicle {
    protected String brand = "Generic Vehicle";

    public void start() {
        System.out.println("Vehicle ignition initiated");
    }

    public void accelerate() {
        System.out.println("Vehicle accelerating at 10 km/h/s");
    }
}

class SportsCar extends Vehicle {
    protected String brand = "Ferrari Turbo"; // Shadows parent field

    @Override
    public void accelerate() {
        System.out.println("SportsCar launching: 0-100 in 2.9 seconds!");
    }

    // Child-specific method
    public void activateDriftMode() {
        System.out.println("Drift mode engaged on SportsCar");
    }
}

public class ParentRefChildObjDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Parent Reference -> Child Object
        Vehicle v = new SportsCar();

        // 1. Inherited method: legal
        v.start();

        // 2. Overridden method: dispatches to SportsCar's version!
        v.accelerate();

        // 3. Child-specific method: COMPILE ERROR if uncommented!
        // v.activateDriftMode(); // Error: cannot find symbol in Vehicle

        // 4. Field access: resolved by Reference Type (not polymorphic!)
        System.out.println("Brand via v: " + v.brand); // Prints 'Generic Vehicle'
    }
}

